  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nIn the wake of a cold front surface high pressure will build into the\nGreat Lakes becoming centered over Lower Michigan by sunrise Saturday\nmorning. Northeast low level flow around this high will advect drier\nair into the region. Lingering mid level clouds across the southern\ncounties will clear from northwest to southeast with lows ranging\nfrom the upper 40s to the lower 50s. \n\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nWesterly flow aloft with surface high pressure building across the\nGreat Lakes today. In a dry air mass temperatures will be on the \ncool side with highs today from the upper 60s north to the lower 70s \nalong and south of the Ohio River. These readings will be around 5 \ndegrees below normal. \n\nAhead of a weak system moving through the Great Lakes a brief\nmoderation to near normal temperatures will be observed on Sunday.\nIn the wake of this system the mid level flow veers northwesterly \nwith Canadian high pressure builds southeast into the region early \nnext week. High temperatures will be mainly in the 60s Monday and \nTuesday - around 7 to 10 degrees below normal. Lows look to be around\n10 degrees below normal with the potential for patchy frost Monday \nnight and Tuesday night. A weak cold front drops south into the area \nlate Wednesday into Thursday but loses its identity. With a dry air \nmass in place only expect an increase in clouds. Readings moderate to\nnear or slightly above normal for the end of the week into next \nweekend as mid level ridging builds north into the Ohio Valley.",
